{
  "title": "Why bridges have gaps",
  "palette": {
    "ink": "#0f1220",
    "paper": "#f3eadb",
    "bg": "#0f1220",
    "hot": "#ffc93c"
  },
  "lines": [
    {
      "id": "L1",
      "text": "Engineers leave gaps in every bridge, on purpose."
    },
    {
      "id": "L2",
      "text": "Pip, a brand new bridge inspector, found one on his very first day. A gap right across the road, wide enough to swallow a phone. So he did what any good inspector would do. He filled it."
    },
    {
      "id": "L3",
      "text": "You see, steel grows when it gets hot. Not a lot. About 1 millimeter for every 10 meters, every 8 degrees. Sounds tiny. But a 100 meter bridge, from a winter night to a summer afternoon, can grow 5 centimeters longer. With no gap, that steel has nowhere to go. So it pushes. Railway tracks can buckle like this on hot days."
    },
    {
      "id": "L4",
      "text": "That's why bridges get expansion joints. Steel teeth that slide past each other. Open in winter, closed in summer. The bridge breathes."
    },
    {
      "id": "L5",
      "text": "Pip's bridge did not breathe. By July, the road had a bump. Pip now inspects tunnels. He says nothing ever grows down there, except his paperwork."
    }
  ],
